{0 Testing and limits}

{1 Testing a worker}

The workero repository tests its own worker with [workerd test]. You can do the
same with yours. It runs the real wasm in the real runtime, so [Promise.await],
[fetch] and bindings all work.

+ Build the worker: [workero build src/main.bc.wasm.js --out _worker-test].
+ Write a [workerd] config. It lists the files of [_worker-test] as modules
  ([.js] as [esModule], [.wasm] as [wasm]) and binds the worker to a test
  worker as a service.
+ In the test worker, call the service, as [env.WORKER.fetch(url)], and compare
  status, headers and body. [env.WORKER.scheduled({ cron, scheduledTime })]
  runs the cron handler.
+ Run [workerd test config.capnp].

[test/worker/run.sh] in the repository generates the config and runs the tests.
[test/worker/main.ml] is the worker, and [test/worker/tests.mjs] has the cases.
Set [globalOutbound] to a small worker to answer {!Workero.fetch} calls
without the network.

[workerd] must be on [PATH]. [opam install workerd], [npm install workerd] or
[wrangler] provides it.

An exception that escapes a handler fails the call on a service binding. A
real HTTP request gets status 500 instead.

For code that does not wait, such as parsing with {!Workero.URL}, write unit
tests. Build them with [(modes js)] and run them with [dune test]: alcotest
runs in node. Node has no JSPI, so code that calls {!Workero.Promise.await}
cannot run there.

{1 Limits}

Cloudflare limits a worker to 64 MiB uncompressed. It sets no compressed
limit. See the
{{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/limits/#worker-size}Workers limits}.
The example workers upload 96 to 123 KiB (31 to 40 KiB gzip).

[examples/limits] pushes the CPU and memory limits with a Mandelbrot route
and an allocation route. In our run, 248 MB of allocation worked and 256 MB
failed.
